From: XI14 <https://github.com/DavidFB-creator/MimOS>
Subject: [PATCH] users: keep the password row usable at 200% scaling

Each password row packs four widgets into one QHBoxLayout: two QLineEdits with a
200px minimum width, a fixed 24x24 status icon, and an error label with a 100px
minimum. That is 524 device-independent pixels of minimum width before layout
spacing.

Qt multiplies those minimums by the device pixel ratio, so at 200% the row
demands roughly 1048px plus three inter-widget gaps, inside a window the test
matrix only guarantees to be 1024px wide. The row's minimum cannot be satisfied,
so it overflows: the status icon is drawn on top of the confirm-password field
and the validation message runs past the right edge of the page. Both were found
by visual review at 200%; a node-tree audit reported no problem, because every
widget still existed and was still named.

Only the password rows are affected. Full name, username and hostname each hold
one QLineEdit plus the icon and the message, coming to 324dip, which fits.

Moving the error label to its own row below the inputs takes 100dip -- 200px at
200% -- and one gap out of the row, bringing it to 424dip and 848px. It also
hands the message the full page width to wrap into, which it is already
configured to do with wordWrap, and puts the validation text under the field it
describes rather than beside it competing for horizontal space.

The root password row gets the same treatment. MimOS hides those fields, so the
defect is not reachable in this distribution, but they are the same widgets with
the same arithmetic, and leaving one of two identical rows fixed is how a defect
comes back.

200% is not a corner case: it is the scale a low-vision user chooses.

Reported upstream by MimOS (XI14). See ADR-050 and ADR-059 in that project.
